{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וַיְהִי כָל הָאָרֶץ שָׂפָה אֶחָת וּדְבָרִים אֲחָדִים.</b> It appears that the entire episode of the dispersion follows the path stated by our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 71b): 'The assembly of the wicked is bad for them and bad for the world; the dispersion of the wicked is good for them and good for the world' — and for the righteous, everything is the reverse. For the wicked, by assembling together, plot evil counsels against others; and in addition, there are drawn among them words of strife, dissension, and hatred — and this is the way of the jealousies found among factions whose every deed is not for the sake of Heaven, but only to make a name for themselves on earth. And the root cause of this is that each of them has a different external purpose; their hearts are divided one from the other — for this one covets wealth and honor, and that one abundance of food, and that one abundance of carnal relations, and this one says 'Let us make ourselves a name' and wants to be higher than the next higher one. And from this the quarrel gropes its way and comes — both from the side that they have no single purpose, for the evil paths are many and the good path is but one, and from the side that each one has desire and will to be in a position of superiority and elevation above his fellow. And this trait, is it not exceedingly prevalent among the multitude of our people? Therefore the dispersion of such factions is better than their assembly, as it is written (Psalms 92:10), 'יִתְפָּרְדוּ כָּל פֹּעֲלֵי אָוֶן' ('all workers of iniquity shall be scattered'). There is no good except to give breathing room between the adherents, as it is written (Psalms 55:10), 'בַּלַּע ה׳ פַּלַּג לְשׁוֹנָם, כִּי רָאִיתִי חָמָס וְרִיב בָּעִיר' ('Destroy, O HaShem, divide their tongues, for I have seen violence and strife in the city')." "But the assembly of the righteous is good, for a single purpose unites them, for they have but one purpose, and through it they become as one — as it is written (Psalms 119:165), 'שָׁלוֹם רָב לְאוֹהֲבֵי תוֹרָתֶךָ' ('Great peace have they who love Your Torah') — but not those who love external purposes in and of themselves. And this is the reason for the dispersion: for at first they were all one language and one speech, and there was peace among them. But they feared that through multiplying upon the earth they would be scattered, for the land would not bear them to dwell together in one place, and wars would ensue — for war is always found between the inhabitants of one country and the inhabitants of another. Therefore they said: behold, now we are one people with one language, and there is reason to fear lest we be scattered of necessity each from his fellow, and the brotherhood among us be severed. Therefore let us build a tower whose head is in the heavens and let us make ourselves a name — for so is the way of the world, that every man has desire and longing to come to dwell in a great city, a gathering-place of many people, and then we shall be one people and none shall be scattered from his brother. And in truth, had they not said 'וְנַעֲשֶׂה לָּנוּ שֵׁם' ('and let us make ourselves a name'), they would have spoken well, and would have attained the ultimate of peace by means of this tower. However, in saying 'וְנַעֲשֶׂה לָּנוּ שֵׁם,' they revealed all the counsels of their heart — for their intent was that their name would go out into the world, and that lands would be called by the names of their men of standing, and it was revealed before HaShem, blessed be He, that because they seek to acquire a name in the world, they will come to the very opposite of what they intended." "Therefore, 'וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ הֵן עַם אֶחָד וְשָׂפָה אַחַת' etc. (Genesis 11:6) — HaShem says: in this scheme which they have devised, they themselves are mistaken, and they will fall into the snare of the very thing from which they are fleeing. For they think that through being scattered they will be divided into many nations and the thread of peace will be cut, for wars will be many; whereas by dwelling in one place they will be one people and peace will be mediates among them. This would be fine if they had not said 'וְנַעֲשֶׂה לָּנוּ שֵׁם' (Genesis 11:4). But in saying this thing, HaShem descended to the end of their reasoning, for everything would be the reverse of what they thought. And on the contrary: they are one people — but when? When they are dispersed and scattered, so that no one has any dealings with his fellow — then they will be one people. But if they all gather to one place to flee from the war of nation against nation, they will fall into a war greater than that — and it is an internal war, every man's sword against his fellow — for the assembly of the wicked is bad for them. And since they said 'וְנַעֲשֶׂה לָּנוּ שֵׁם,' each one will want to lord it over his fellow and be higher than him, for this is found among factions who do all their deeds for the sake of broadcasting their name. Therefore it is good to scatter them and prevent them from this building. This is what is said: 'וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ הֵן עַם אֶחָד וְשָׂפָה אַחַת לְכֻלָּם' — for now, after they are somewhat scattered, they are one people and one language, for each one says 'הֵן' to what his fellow says 'הֵן,' and 'לָאו' to what his fellow says 'לָאו' — for there is not so much jealousy and competition among them, since they have not yet pursued the acquisition of a name. 'וְזֶה הַחִלָּם לַעֲשׂוֹת' (Genesis 11:6) — this tower, will be the beginning of the very thing from which they think to escape, and it will be the reverse of their intent. And it will be the beginning of their doing what they did not do before, for before this there was peace among them, and now there will be every man's hand against his fellow — on account of the appointments and the governance that all inhabitants of a great city require, for without them each man would swallow his fellow alive. And since their desire is to make a name for themselves, each one will want to be ruler and prince in this great city, so that his name goes out through all the world — and through this, words of strife will multiply among them, as has been said." "<b>וְעַתָּה לֹא יִבָּצֵר מֵהֶם כֹּל אֲשֶׁר יָזְמוּ לַעֲשׂוֹת.</b> Its meaning is not in the language of wonderment, but in truth He said so — for since they intended to establish a foundation for the preservation of peace, I too think the same; only, through an inverse cause from what they thought. They thought to establish peace through their assembly, believing that their assembly was good for them; and I see to establish peace through dispersion, for dispersion is better for them than assembly. Therefore, 'הָבָה נֵרְדָה וְנָבְלָה שָׁם שְׂפָתָם' (Genesis 11:7) — for through this they will be scattered of necessity, and then it is good for them and for all the world, for there is never peace among people whose every aim is to make a name for themselves on earth — and the children of our people are proof of this." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma, Noach 18) that they said: not all of us are agreed that the Supernal One should be chosen for Himself, etc. They thereby indicated to what extent the minds of men of name can reach — to the point that even the Shekhinah is accounted as nothing before him. For at first their tongue walks the earth seeking greatness for itself, and afterward they set their mouth even in the heavens to challenge the kingdom of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it were. And now, come and see how diminished this trait is — does it not destroy two Temples and cause the delay of the building of the Third Temple, until He removes from among us the exultant of pride and there remains a poor and lowly people, taking refuge in the name of HaShem (Zephaniah 3:12)?"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:14.395389443Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}